    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e4",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_13",
      "quote": "The difference in pain intensity scores (or in pain relief) between patients who were administered opioids versus those not administered opioids from baseline to cutoff time as specified in the original RCT (the final single time point from each follow- up were used for the main analysis), adverse events incidence, utilization of rescue analgesics, and data related to patient demographics were reported for each study. If data were available for at least three studies, results for the main outcome and for adverse events were pooled and meta- analyzed. Sensitivity analyses were performed for studies with available short- term follow- up (≤3 days) to examine whether opioids were more effective in this situation. Another sensitivity analysis compared patients treated with codeine versus those treated with other opioids since codeine is a prodrug and its analgesic properties depend on its conversion to morphine, which varies considerably between subjects. Sensitivity analyses were also performed excluding studies with high risk of bias and for studies involving a pediatric population compared to adults.",
      "reason": "Groups for synthesis (opioid vs non-opioid) and subgroups for sensitivity analyses are specified and linked to the review's comparisons."
    },
